"# aos-gdb: stop an agent when a condition holds, look, go on\n\n`aos-gdb NAME` opens Python with `import aos.gdb as g` and `a = g.attach(NAME)` done (`--daemon D` or `$AOS_DAEMON`). A trigger is an ordinary hook on that agent (its own `<stack>/hooks.json`, name `gdb_...`, handler `lib/tools/agent/gdb_trigger`). A watcher is a separate proc on the daemon table (name `gdbw_...`) that looks at the agent's files and pauses it.\n\nHow it stops depends on the event, not on an option:\n\n- `before_tool`, `before_queue` (not happened yet): the trigger **holds** the agent inside its hook. The tool has not run; the table still says running. `a.resume()` lets that call run as it was; `a.resume(skip=True)` skips the tool (the brain gets a \"skipped at a breakpoint\" line); `aos-agent ctl resume` from a shell also lets it run.\n- other events (`after_tool`, `brain_failed`, ...) and watchers: `aos-agent ctl pause`; the loop stops after the current step (after_tool: the result is in history, the brain has not seen it). `a.resume()` = `ctl resume`.\n\n```python\na.stop_before_tool(\"write\")                 # held before write runs\na.stop_after_tool(\"write\"); a.stop_on_tool_error(times=2)\na.stop_when(\"after_tool\", sh=\"grep -q secret || exit 10\")        # event JSON on stdin; exit 0 = hit, 10 = no hit\na.stop_when(\"before_tool\", py=lambda ev: ev[\"arguments\"].get(\"path\", \"\").endswith(\".env\"), once=True)\na.watch(py=lambda t: len(t.history(0)) > 20, every_ms=500)          # t = the target's files; t.mem = a dict kept between looks\ng.watch([\"ann\", \"bob\"], sh=\"test -f flag || exit 10\", times=3)      # one watcher, two agents; cwd = each target's stack\na.say(\"hi\"); a.wait_paused(); a.why(); a.held(); a.where(); a.history(3)\na.resume(); a.triggers(); a.remove(\"before_tool_1\"); a.clear(); a.watchers(); a.unwatch(\"w1\")\n```\n\n- While held: letters still queue; a `ctl pause` or `ctl stop` sent meanwhile applies after the tool ran (`why()` says so). `ctl sleep`, `rm` or a daemon shutdown cut the hold: the tool did not run, and the same call is held again when the agent comes back.\n- Watchers are one step late (they poll). `times` = rounds in a row; after a stop the condition must be seen false once before it can stop again; paused or held targets are not checked. A broken watcher condition is `last_error` in `watchers()`; a dead watcher does not touch its targets.\n- Triggers use `--on-error continue`: a broken condition or handler is one warning line, the agent does not stop or restart. `why()` lists notes since the last resume (`by`: hook, watch, repl, ctl) and whether they belong to this stop.\n- Python conditions run in another process: they see `json`, `re`, `os` and builtins; pass other values as defaults (`lambda ev, x=x: ...`). They are refused at once when they use outer variables or unknown names.\n- Do not put triggers or watchers on another agent unless you were asked to debug it: it stops that agent (aos/rules.json). More: `aos-gdb --help`, `help(a)` in the REPL, proto8/py/aos/apps/gdb/README.md.\n"
